      "message": "eval answers a macro-made `(library ...)` test from a stale cache after the loader gains the library.\n\nFirst `(eval \u0027(let-syntax ((m (syntax-rules () ((_ lib) (cond-expand ((library lib) \u0027yes) (else \u0027no)))))) (m (opt feature))) (environment \u0027(scheme base)))` with a loader lacking `(opt feature)` answers `no` and keeps `(opt feature) -\u003e false` in `expander.available` (line 495); a second identical eval after the loader starts serving `(opt feature)` still answers `no`, because line 482 returns the cached answer without asking the resolver again. I ran this as a scratch-only test (`load_with` with a mutable loader, two `cx.call`s around `loader.add`): first call `no` (correct), second call `no` (wrong, should be `yes`). `forget()` truncates units but not `available`, and `prepare` only extends it, so the stale answer lasts for the evaluator\u0027s lifetime; importing the library in between does not help either, since `has_library` on line 485 is shadowed by the stale entry. The `vali` REPL evaluates every input through this same evaluator with a file-backed host loader (`repl.rs` via `load_with`), so adding a library file mid-session keeps testing as missing.\n\nFix: reset the per-evaluation answer cache in `prepare` (clear `available` before `add_available(graph.available)`, keeping the within-expansion inserts for determinism), or at minimum check `has_library` before `available` so a compiled library wins over a cached miss.",
